":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"self-loop (XGI’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DiHypergraph"},{"text":" allows both).","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/directedhypergraph"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/MutableDirectedGraph":{"role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A bidirectional directed graph that generic code can mutate: Boost’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"MutableGraph"},{"type":"text","text":" concept"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"add_vertex"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"add_edge"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"remove_edge"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"remove_vertex"},{"type":"text","text":"), for a directed representation. It exists so that a wrapper which adds a rule, such as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedAcyclicGraph"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"can own any of the mutable representations as its base and still run at the base’s speed."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/MutableDirectedGraph","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MutableDirectedGraph"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"